*(
    title: Vectors in Three Dimensions
    description: Adding a third component, and what carries over unchanged from two dimensions.
    tags: vectors, three dimensions, magnitude, unit vector, components, extension 2, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Almost everything Extension 1 established about vectors in the plane survives the move to space untouched. Addition is still tip to tail, scalar multiplication still stretches without turning, and the magnitude is still a Pythagoras calculation with one more term under the root.

    Written out here: component and column notation, the basis vectors, magnitude and distance between two points in space, unit vectors, and the arithmetic of addition and scalar multiplication in three components.

    What does not carry over is as important. In the plane, two directions are either parallel or they meet; in space they can do neither. The first tool for telling such cases apart is the one product of two vectors that returns a number, set out in *The Dot Product*.

    internote(ref: the-dot-product.chalk)
}
